\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Elimination with orthogonal perfect pairings}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{Let #{X,Y} be unital modules over a commutative unital ring #{R},
with \vocabk{chosen perfect symmetric pairings}{fdip-001O}
#{b_X,b_Y}. On the [chosen sum](fdip-001T) #{M=X\oplus Y},
choose the orthogonal sum form
##{b_M((x,y),(x',y'))=b_X(x,x')+b_Y(y,y').}
It is perfect and symmetric, and its injection/projection adjoints are
##{\iota_X^\dagger=\pi_X,\quad \pi_X^\dagger=\iota_X,\qquad
   \iota_Y^\dagger=\pi_Y,\quad \pi_Y^\dagger=\iota_Y.}
Suppose #{Q:M\to M} is [self-adjoint](fdip-001P), with components
#{A,B,E,C} and invertible #{C}. Then
##{A^\dagger=A,\quad C^\dagger=C,\quad E=B^\dagger,\qquad
   S^\dagger=S\quad\text{for }S=A-BC^{-1}E.}
For the [triangular factors](fdip-001U),
##{V^\dagger=U,\qquad Q=V^\dagger DV,\qquad
   D(x,y)=(Sx,Cy).}
Consequently, with #{K=C^{-1}E},
##{\begin{aligned}
b_M((x,y),Q(x,y))
 &=b_X(x,Sx)\\
 &\quad+b_Y(y+Kx,C(y+Kx)).
\end{aligned}}}

\proof{
\p{Bilinearity and symmetry of #{b_M} follow termwise. For a linear
functional #{\ell:M\to R}, represent #{\ell\iota_X} and
#{\ell\iota_Y} uniquely by #{x_\ell\in X,y_\ell\in Y} using the
two perfect pairings. Then for every #{(x,y)},
##{\ell(x,y)=\ell\iota_X(x)+\ell\iota_Y(y)
 =b_X(x,x_\ell)+b_Y(y,y_\ell).}
This gives a representative #{(x_\ell,y_\ell)}. Uniqueness follows by
testing #{(x,0)} and #{(0,y)} separately. The representative depends
linearly on #{\ell}, since the two inverse duality maps are linear.
Thus the currying map of #{b_M} is a linear isomorphism.}

\p{For example,
#{b_M(\iota_Xx,(u,v))=b_X(x,u)=b_X(x,\pi_X(u,v))}.
[Adjoint uniqueness and involution](fdip-001P) give the two #{X}
identities, and the same calculation gives those for #{Y}.
Adjointing the definitions of the four components and using
#{Q^\dagger=Q} gives #{A^\dagger=A}, #{C^\dagger=C} and
#{B^\dagger=E}. The inverse-adjoint law then gives
#{(C^{-1})^\dagger=C^{-1}} and
##{K^\dagger=(C^{-1}E)^\dagger=BC^{-1},\qquad
   (BC^{-1}E)^\dagger=BC^{-1}E.}
The latter identity proves #{S^\dagger=S}.}

\p{For #{z=(x,y)} and #{w=(u,v)},
##{\begin{aligned}
b_M(Vz,w)
 &=b_X(x,u)+b_Y(y+Kx,v)\\
 &=b_X(x,u+K^\dagger v)+b_Y(y,v)\\
 &=b_M(z,Uw).
\end{aligned}}
Uniqueness gives #{V^\dagger=U}; factorization yields #{Q=V^\dagger DV}.
Finally #{b_M(z,Qz)=b_M(Vz,DVz)}, and expanding the orthogonal sum
form gives the stated identity.}
}

\p{This is a direct deduction from chosen dualities and triangular
factorization. The real symmetric completion in
\citet{Appendix A.5.5, p. 650}{boyd2004convex} is its real coordinate
specialization. The chosen orthogonal sum is essential: an arbitrary
perfect form on #{M} need not have these injection/projection adjoints.
No division by two or recovery of bilinear data from diagonal values
is used; the identity remains valid in characteristic two.}

\p{TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{LinearMap.BilinForm.prod} gives the
orthogonal product form, and
\leanref/tauceti{LinearMap.BilinForm.nondegenerate_prod_iff} identifies
its radical nondegeneracy. The functional-splitting proof above also
proves the surjectivity required for perfection. These declarations
alone do not assert the paired-congruence result.}
